Diagnose Codex failure after SSH recovery

Codex 0.163.0 leaves its TUI alive after its reconnect budget expires,
while rumpelpod continues reattaching to that frontend. Preserve an
opt-in SSH outage diagnostic so recovery strategies can be evaluated
against the real frontend, including draft retention and thread resume.

The diagnostic compares a short outage with one exceeding the retry
budget, checks that git sync and pod entry recover independently, then
checks stale frontend reattachment and replacement frontend recovery.
It is ignored by default because it describes a known failure and
deliberately waits beyond Codex's two-minute deadline.

Validated with Codex 0.163.0 on Linux: the diagnostic passed in 197s.
The four required smoke tests also passed through cargo pipeline.
This branch contains investigation coverage, not a runtime recovery fix.

Run it with:
  cargo pipeline --test-threads 1 \
    codex_frontend_stays_offline_after_ssh_recovers -- --ignored

Inspected upstream Codex rust-v0.163.0 at
83086188ae0614bd882818ff1aa23f3194962012 and main at
46d252f8bf8b09f4ef765bc72e40f02c229da3e6:

- tui/src/app/reconnect.rs uses a 120-second shared deadline with
  delays of 0, 1, 2, 4, then 8 seconds between attempts.
- tui/src/app/startup.rs records reconnect.failed and stops scheduling
  retries, but continues running the TUI.
- tui/src/chatwidget/reconnect.rs preserves editable drafts and tells
  the user to copy them before quitting and relaunching.
- The disconnect event is internal to app-server-client; the inspected
  CLI options and hooks expose no reconnect-exhaustion notification or
  exit-on-disconnect policy.

Prefer an upstream option to keep reconnecting, or an explicit frontend
lifecycle event/exit policy with draft preservation. Without that,
rumpelpod can supervise frontend replacement after its own transport
recovers, but must account for local-only drafts, resume the correct
thread, and avoid replaying requests whose results were lost.
